schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Web3 as addition, not replacement, to existing mission-driven work
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A framing that positions web3 tooling as analogous to "your website" — a
    tool that supports existing work (community relationships, program
    delivery, physical presence) without replacing it. The recommended
    adoption pattern: identify a specific current pain point (fundraising,
    transparency, coordination, or community building), then try one small
    web3-native intervention rather than a wholesale rebuild.
  toolkit_usage: >-
    The organizing idea of the whole article; every subsequent
    recommendation (pick one pain point, start small, keep what works) follows
    directly from this framing.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - >-
      "Tool, not strategy" adoption framing common in general
      digital-transformation guidance (not named in the article, but the same
      underlying pattern)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      "The goal is impact, not technology for its own sake" — the article is
      explicit that web3 adoption is instrumental to mission, not a goal in
      itself.
  tensions: []
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    None flagged — a general adoption-strategy framing, not attributed to a
    specific community or tradition. Note: this article is short (~380 words)
    and generic, with no named organizations, tools beyond a passing mention
    of Snapshot/POAP, or case studies — the concept-lineage here captures the
    article's full argument rather than a deeper tradition.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/relates-to-existing-work.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded: this is one of the shortest and most generic articles
# in the batch (378 estimated words per its own frontmatter, research_done:
# false). No named organizations, funding data, or verifiable claims exist to
# extract as separate resource or claim-evidence objects beyond the two
# candidates filed here (encyclopedia-entry + this concept-lineage).
